KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ cerr good: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ cerr precision: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ cerr unitbuf: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ cerr tied to cout: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ cout precision: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.6\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
std::cerr and std::cout are defined and constructed in the compiled
library, and the model of ios_base::Init has an empty body, so cbmc gives
them nondeterministic contents, vtable pointers included: every assertion
fails, and output through them reports dereference failures (the cause of
the iostream tests tagged no-vacuity-check).
